ORGO.reg({"types":{"nuc-pairs":{"id":"nuc-pairs","title":"Circle the stronger nucleophile","exam":"Q1C","points":15,"asks":"Two species are shown. Circle the one that attacks carbon faster in an {{SN2}} reaction.","know":[{"h":"<b>Nucleophilicity is a rate</b>: how fast a species displaces a leaving group in an {{SN2}} reaction. Basicity is an equilibrium ({{pKa}} of the conjugate acid). They often agree, but not always.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 2"},{"h":"<b>The lecture table</b> (relative {{SN2}} rates): {{HC≡C-}} 126,000; {{HS-}} 126,000; {{I-}} 80,000; {{HO-}} 16,000; {{N3-}} 8,000; {{NH3}} 8,000; {{Cl-}} 1,000; {{CH3CO2-}} 630; {{CH3OH}} 1; {{H2O}} 1.","from":"Table 7.5"},{"h":"<b>Across a row</b>, nucleophilicity follows basicity: it increases to the left ({{H2N-}} > {{HO-}} > {{F-}}).","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 3"},{"h":"<b>Down a column</b>, nucleophilicity increases while basicity decreases ({{I-}} > {{Br-}} > {{Cl-}}; {{HS-}} > {{HO-}}): larger atoms are more polarizable and less tightly solvated.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 3"},{"h":"<b>Charge</b>: an anion is a better nucleophile than its neutral conjugate acid ({{HO-}} 16,000 against {{H2O}} 1).","from":"Table 7.5"},{"h":"<b>Bulk</b>: with the same attacking atom, the bulkier species is the poorer nucleophile. tert-Butoxide is a strong base but a poor nucleophile.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 2 to 3"},{"h":"<b>Resonance</b>: a charge spread over several atoms is less available. Acetate (630) is far weaker than hydroxide (16,000).","from":"Table 7.5"}],"method":["Find the atom that attacks in each species.","Same atom? The one with the negative charge wins; if both are charged, the less bulky and less delocalized one wins.","Different atoms in the same row? The one further left (the stronger base) wins.","Different atoms in the same column? The lower one wins, although it is the weaker base.","Check your answer against the lecture table if both species are on it."],"pitfalls":[{"h":"Choosing the stronger base when the atoms are in the same column (choosing {{Cl-}} over {{I-}}, or {{HO-}} over {{HS-}})."},{"h":"Choosing tert-butoxide because it is the stronger base: bulk makes it the weaker nucleophile."}],"links":{"read":["sn2-2"],"lab":["nucleophiles"]}}},"items":{"nuc-pairs-s01":{"id":"nuc-pairs-s01","type":"nuc-pairs","role":"worked","kind":"mcq","diff":1,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q1C(i)","layout":"pair","stem":"Circle the stronger nucleophile.","choices":[{"h":"[[mol:[I-]]]","ok":true,"why":"Iodide is lower in the column: larger, more polarizable, and held less tightly by a protic solvent. Lecture table: 80,000."},{"h":"[[mol:[Cl-]]]","ok":false,"why":"Chloride is the stronger base but the weaker nucleophile. Lecture table: 1,000, eighty times slower than iodide."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Find the attacking atoms","h":"I and Cl. Same charge, same column of the periodic table.","ask":{"q":"Same row or same column?","a":"Same column (the halogens)."}},{"t":"Use the column trend","h":"Down a column nucleophilicity <b>increases</b>, even though basicity decreases. The bigger atom's electrons are easier to push toward carbon, and a protic solvent solvates the small ion more tightly.","ask":{"q":"Which way does basicity run in a column?","a":"Up: chloride is the stronger base. Nucleophilicity runs the other way."}},{"t":"Check against the table","h":"{{I-}} 80,000 against {{Cl-}} 1,000."}],"answer":"{{I-}}","rule":"Down a column: bigger atom, better nucleophile."}}},"mols":{"[I-]":{"w":14,"h":11,"p":[],"t":[[0,1.8,9.3,10,"I"],[0,7.6,5.3,6,"-"]],"a":[[4.3,4.3,"I",-1,4,0,0]],"b":[]},"[Cl-]":{"w":16,"h":11,"p":[],"t":[[0,2.5,9.3,10,"C"],[0,9.4,9.3,10,"l"],[0,11.5,5.3,6,"-"]],"a":[[10.3,4.3,"Cl",-1,4,0,0]],"b":[]}}});
